ildren":[{"type":"text","value":"Arrow and link endpoint channels are defined in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-thresholds-and-bands","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add thresholds and bands"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a rule for a single semantic value:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zero"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Target"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Regulatory threshold"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Average or benchmark"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Current time"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a ranged area or rectangle when the annotation is an interval:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Acceptable range"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Forecast uncertainty"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maintenance window"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Selected time span"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Name the threshold or band in surrounding text or a sparse text layer. A\ndecorative grid line should not compete with a policy boundary that carries\nmeaning."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"select-annotations-in-data-preparation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Select annotations in data preparation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prepare or select a small annotation dataset:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Select observations by a documented rule such as minimum, maximum, first\nthreshold crossing, or named event."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep the original datum and stable ID when the source row already carries\nthe annotation's semantic coordinates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put presentation-only label formatting, anchors, and x/y offsets on the\ntext mark. Add fields only when they are reusable annotation data."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render dots, rules, links, and text as independent marks."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This makes the selection auditable. The chart renderer should not decide which\nbusiness events are interesting."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/data-and-channels.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data and Channels"}]},{"type":"text","value":" covers mixed mark sources\nand typed accessors."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"use-html-overlays-only-when-necessary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use HTML overlays only when necessary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An application overlay is appropriate for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rich interactive content"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Buttons, links, forms, or menus"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A persistent details panel"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A nested chart"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Product chrome that should not be serialized into SVG"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anchor it from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.chart"},{"type":"text","value":" or a focused "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint"},{"type":"text","value":", then keep its state and\nlifecycle in the application. Do not mutate chart SVG to create a second,\nunreconciled state model."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for rich tooltip\nownership and "},{"type":"link","href":"./interactive-charts.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactive Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for complete\napplication-controlled examples."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every annotation answers a specific reader question."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Selection rules are prepared and testable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Labels include units or period meaning where needed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text collision is verified at the smallest container."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Offsets remain relative to semantic coordinates after resize."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Color is not the only distinction between data and annotation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decorative geometry emits no fake interaction points."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic layers have stable mark IDs and datum keys."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rich overlays are keyboard reachable, contained, and destroyed on unmount."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text channel and positioning options are in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/text-frame-and-facet.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text, Frame, and Facet"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-annotation","text":"Choose the annotation","level":2},{"id":"label-before-and-after-change","text":"Label before-and-after change","level":2},{"id":"show-two-dimensional-movement","text":"Show two-dimensional movement","level":2},{"id":"add-thresholds-and-bands","text":"Add thresholds and bands","level":2},{"id":"select-annotations-in-data-preparation","text":"Select annotations in data preparation","level":2},{"id":"use-html-overlays-only-when-necessary","text":"Use HTML overlays only when necessary","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nAnnotations explain selected evidence. They should name a threshold, event,\nendpoint, difference, or unusual observation that matters to the reader.\n\nTreat an annotation as data whenever possible. A prepared row with semantic\ncoordinates survives resizing, scale changes, server rendering, and updates\nmore reliably than a hand-positioned SVG fragment.\n\n## Choose the annotation\n\n| Reader question                                        | Start with                             |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------- |\n| How did each category change between two periods?      | Slopegraph with direct endpoint labels |\n| In which direction did a point move in two dimensions? | Change arrow                           |\n| Where is a target, zero, or policy boundary?           | Rule plus concise text                 |\n| Which time span needs explanation?                     | Explicit interval rectangle or area    |\n| Which few observations are notable?                    | Selected dots and text                 |\n| Does the reader need arbitrary HTML or controls?       | Application-owned overlay              |\n\n[Marks and Layering](../concepts/marks-and-layering.md) is the source of truth\nfor render order and stable layer identity.\n\n## Label before-and-after change\n\nA slopegraph gives both periods a shared quantitative scale, connects each\ncategory's endpoints, and labels the values directly.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=30-slopegraph height=480 -->\n\nPreserve category identity for the links and endpoints; supply `key` only when\nthe mark cannot infer it. Direct labels remove a legend lookup, but they need\ncollision policy when values converge. Filter to meaningful categories,\nincrease vertical space, or use an accessible detail view rather than allowing\nunreadable overlap.\n\nA slope implies before-to-after order. Label both periods and keep the same\nquantitative scale.\n\n## Show two-dimensional movement\n\nA change arrow connects one quantitative state to another. Position carries\nthe start and end values; the arrowhead carries direction.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=32-change-arrows height=480 -->\n\nKeep both endpoints in the prepared row. Do not infer direction from color or\nrow order inside the renderer. Label the compared states and retain original\nvalues for focus and tooltips.\n\nArrow and link endpoint channels are defined in\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md).\n\n## Add thresholds and bands\n\nUse a rule for a single semantic value:\n\n- Zero\n- Target\n- Regulatory threshold\n- Average or benchmark\n- Current time\n\nUse a ranged area or rectangle when the annotation is an interval:\n\n- Acceptable range\n- Forecast uncertainty\n- Maintenance window\n- Selected time span\n\nName the threshold or band in surrounding text or a sparse text layer. A\ndecorative grid line should not compete with a policy boundary that carries\nmeaning.\n\n## Select annotations in data preparation\n\nPrepare or select a small annotation dataset:\n\n1. Select observations by a documented rule such as minimum, maximum, first\n   threshold crossing, or named event.\n2. Keep the original datum and stable ID when the source row already carries\n   the annotation's semantic coordinates.\n3. Put presentation-only label formatting, anchors, and x/y offsets on the\n   text mark. Add fields only when they are reusable annotation data.\n4. Render dots, rules, links, and text as independent marks.\n\nThis makes the selection auditable. The chart renderer should not decide which\nbusiness events are interesting.\n\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) covers mixed mark sources\nand typed accessors.\n\n## Use HTML overlays only when necessary\n\nAn application overlay is appropriate for:\n\n- Rich interactive content\n- Buttons, links, forms, or menus\n- A persistent details panel\n- A nested chart\n- Product chrome that should not be serialized into SVG\n\nAnchor it from `scene.chart` or a focused `ChartPoint`, then keep its state and\nlifecycle in the application. Do not mutate chart SVG to create a second,\nunreconciled state model.\n\nSee [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md) for rich tooltip\nownership and [Interactive Charts](./interactive-charts.md) for complete\napplication-controlled examples.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Every annotation answers a specific reader question.\n- Selection rules are prepared and testable.\n- Labels include units or period meaning where needed.\n- Text collision is verified at the smallest container.\n- Offsets remain relative to semantic coordinates after resize.\n- Color is not the only distinction between data and annotation.\n- Decorative geometry emits no fake interaction points.\n- Dynamic layers have stable mark IDs and datum keys.\n- Rich overlays are keyboard reachable, contained, and destroyed on unmount.\n\nText channel and positioning options are in\n[Text, Frame, and Facet](../reference/marks/text-frame-and-facet.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Annotations and Overlays\ndescription: Add direct labels, thresholds, change indicators, and explanatory layers without hiding chart semantics.\n---\n\nAnnotations explain selected evidence. They should name a threshold, event,\nendpoint, difference, or unusual observation that matters to the reader.\n\nTreat an annotation as data whenever possible. A prepared row with semantic\ncoordinates survives resizing, scale changes, server rendering, and updates\nmore reliably than a hand-positioned SVG fragment.\n\n## Choose the annotation\n\n| Reader question                                        | Start with                             |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------- |\n| How did each category change between two periods?      | Slopegraph with direct endpoint labels |\n| In which direction did a point move in two dimensions? | Change arrow                           |\n| Where is a target, zero, or policy boundary?           | Rule plus concise text                 |\n| Which time span needs explanation?                     | Explicit interval rectangle or area    |\n| Which few observations are notable?                    | Selected dots and text                 |\n| Does the reader need arbitrary HTML or controls?       | Application-owned overlay              |\n\n[Marks and Layering](../concepts/marks-and-layering.md) is the source of truth\nfor render order and stable layer identity.\n\n## Label before-and-after change\n\nA slopegraph gives both periods a shared quantitative scale, connects each\ncategory's endpoints, and labels the values directly.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=30-slopegraph height=480 -->\n\nPreserve category identity for the links and endpoints; supply `key` only when\nthe mark cannot infer it. Direct labels remove a legend lookup, but they need\ncollision policy when values converge. Filter to meaningful categories,\nincrease vertical space, or use an accessible detail view rather than allowing\nunreadable overlap.\n\nA slope implies before-to-after order. Label both periods and keep the same\nquantitative scale.\n\n## Show two-dimensional movement\n\nA change arrow connects one quantitative state to another. Position carries\nthe start and end values; the arrowhead carries direction.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=32-change-arrows height=480 -->\n\nKeep both endpoints in the prepared row. Do not infer direction from color or\nrow order inside the renderer. Label the compared states and retain original\nvalues for focus and tooltips.\n\nArrow and link endpoint channels are defined in\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md).\n\n## Add thresholds and bands\n\nUse a rule for a single semantic value:\n\n- Zero\n- Target\n- Regulatory threshold\n- Average or benchmark\n- Current time\n\nUse a ranged area or rectangle when the annotation is an interval:\n\n- Acceptable range\n- Forecast uncertainty\n- Maintenance window\n- Selected time span\n\nName the threshold or band in surrounding text or a sparse text layer. A\ndecorative grid line should not compete with a policy boundary that carries\nmeaning.\n\n## Select annotations in data preparation\n\nPrepare or select a small annotation dataset:\n\n1. Select observations by a documented rule such as minimum, maximum, first\n   threshold crossing, or named event.\n2. Keep the original datum and stable ID when the source row already carries\n   the annotation's semantic coordinates.\n3. Put presentation-only label formatting, anchors, and x/y offsets on the\n   text mark. Add fields only when they are reusable annotation data.\n4. Render dots, rules, links, and text as independent marks.\n\nThis makes the selection auditable. The chart renderer should not decide which\nbusiness events are interesting.\n\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) covers mixed mark sources\nand typed accessors.\n\n## Use HTML overlays only when necessary\n\nAn application overlay is appropriate for:\n\n- Rich interactive content\n- Buttons, links, forms, or menus\n- A persistent details panel\n- A nested chart\n- Product chrome that should not be serialized into SVG\n\nAnchor it from `scene.chart` or a focused `ChartPoint`, then keep its state and\nlifecycle in the application. Do not mutate chart SVG to create a second,\nunreconciled state model.\n\nSee [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md) for rich tooltip\nownership and [Interactive Charts](./interactive-charts.md) for complete\napplication-controlled examples.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Every annotation answers a specific reader question.\n- Selection rules are prepared and testable.\n- Labels include units or period meaning where needed.\n- Text collision is verified at the smallest container.\n- Offsets remain relative to semantic coordinates after resize.\n- Color is not the only distinction between data and annotation.\n- Decorative geometry emits no fake interaction points.\n- Dynamic layers have stable mark IDs and datum keys.\n- Rich overlays are keyboard reachable, contained, and destroyed on unmount.\n\nText channel and positioning options are in\n[Text, Frame, and Facet](../reference/marks/text-frame-and-facet.md).\n"}
